Top and bottom types

Type systems often have types representing the largest and smallest possible sets of values. These are called top and bottom types.
Any is top type which accepts everything. And it is possible to access any deep property. Typescript won’t complain even though any property is not there. 
Unknown is another top type. We can accept any value however it has difference. We have to narrow the type to access the papery for example. Values with an unknown type cannot be used without first applying a type guard. Sometimes people refer to this property of unknown by describing it as “opaque”.

For example with if check blocks we can narrow down to the specific type. 

Some examples of top types.  unknown is great for values received at runtime (e.g., your data layer). By obligating consumers of these values to perform some light validation before using them, errors are caught earlier, and can often be surfaced with more context.
Another wise use of unknown is handling throwables in a catch block

When we migrate from JS to TS, a lot of things will be any at first. For example error catch block by default will give any to variable `e` and we can say it unknown to narrow down to the specific type
Also with `useUnknownInCatchVariables: true` will make throwable by default any.




object is almost top type. All possible values except primitives.  Null and undefined is 2 of 7 primitive types and not allowed to assign to object (if strictNullCheck is true).

Almost top type: Empty object {}

The empty object type {} represents the set { all possible values, except for null and undefined }
We can assign string date, number to the variables has type {} empty object.
You can use the type {} in combination with the intersection type operator & to remove nullability from another type


For example, 
type NullableStringOrNumber = string | number | null | undefined;
type StringOrNumber = NullableStringOrNumber & {}
In this way, we can narrow and exclude null and undefineds from variable.


Bottom types:

A bottom type (symbol: ⊥) is a type that describes no possible value allowed by the system. To use our set theory mental model, we could describe this as a type representing the set { } (intentionally empty). 
There is one bottom type in TS: never
It means you shouldn’t reach there, nothing should be left from possible types options. 

We can create error class with never. So it can be error for UnreachableCode(arg: never).


Unit types:

Unit types are types that represent a set of exactly one value. An example of this is a literal type. In TypeScript, the types null and undefined are both unit types.
We can’t exchange them. 

Void is almost unit type, it can check against undefined as well.




Nullish Values

Although null, void and undefined are all used to describe “nothing” or “empty”, they are independent types in TypeScript. 
Null means that there is a value which is nothing.  This nothing is very much a defined value, and is certainly a presence — not an absence — of information.

undefined means the value isn’t available (yet?)

void should exclusively be used to describe that a function’s return value should be ignored

! Is used for not-null assertion. 

The non-null assertion operator (!.) is used to cast away the possibility that a value might be null or undefined.
Keep in mind that the value could still be null or undefined, this operator just tells TypeScript to ignore that possibility.
If the value does turn out to be missing, you will get the familiar cannot call foo on undefined family of errors at runtime:


Definite assignment assertion

The definite assignment !: assertion is used to suppress TypeScript’s objections about a class field being used, when it can’t be proven1 that it was initialized.

If you don’t put it, TS will complain that someone might use it without instantiating. 

